{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before the library closes, when the light turns amber and dust hangs visible in the air. People speak more softly then, as if they already belong partly to the evening outside and partly to the stories still open before them. A student sleeps with a textbook for a pillow, one shoe untied, dreaming probably of formulas that refused to stay awake either. Near the window, an older man copies poems into a notebook with slow, deliberate handwriting, mouthing each line before he trusts it to paper. I like to wander the stacks without looking for anything in particular, letting my fingers read the spines the way other people trail hands in water. Every shelf is a kind of weather system, calm in the atlases, stormy in the histories, bright and unpredictable among the travel guides from decades ago. Sometimes I open a book at random and find a margin note left by someone I will never meet, arguing gently with a dead author. That feels like the truest kind of conversation, unhurried, unanswered, and somehow still alive across all that distance. When the librarian finally dims the lights, we gather our coats and unfinished thoughts and step out into streets that seem slightly rewritten. The city has not changed, of course, but our attention has, and attention changes everything it touches for a while. I walk home slower then, carrying borrowed worlds, grateful for doors that close gently behind us every single night.",
